digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-55907-w
  rankdir="TB";
  "N1" [label="(1,0,0) Capillary waves are surface-tension-dominated mechanical perturbations of a liquid–gas interface."];
  "N10" [label="(28,0,0) Currently, the plastron may be lost (Wenzel state), and partially wet configurations exist (intermediate states)."];
  "N11" [label="(29,0,0) Currently, plastron condition is typically assessed optically (bright-field microscopy, total internal reflection, confocal)."];
  "N12" [label="(30,0,0) Currently, acoustics-based alternatives have limitations: transducer integration complexity/cost and insufficient access to plastron dynamics and intermediate wetting states."];
  "N13" [label="(31,0,0) Mechanical waves carry properties of their propagation medium, so they can be used as probes in hard-to-measure media."];
  "N16" [label="(0,0,0) If interface boundary conditions/stiffness change (e.g., via pinning/constraints), then the dispersion relation and wave speed/frequency response will change in a measurable way."];
  "N17" [label="(0,0,0) If a measurable wave feature depends on interface state, then measuring that wave feature can be used to infer/monitor the interface state remotely."];
  "N18" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given systematic studies are lacking and superhydrophobicity can facilitate microstructure-pinned interfaces, therefore a controllable microstructure-pinned gas–water interface (plastron) with excited capillary waves is a plausible way to address the gap."];
  "N19" [label="(33,0,0) Currently, capillary waves are induced by ultrasound and travel on a gas–water interface bounded to solid microstructures of a superhydrophobic plastron."];
  "N2" [label="(16,0,0) Currently, pinning the water interface to channel walls exerts an additional restoring force that stiffens the interface and increases wave propagation speed."];
  "N20" [label="(34,0,0) Currently, wave behavior will be studied by varying (i) three-phase contact-line density and (ii) pillar height."];
  "N20c" [label="(0,0,0) Currently, contact-line density and pillar height are controllable microstructure/pinning parameters of a plastron interface."];
  "N23" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given shallow-water no-slip and channel-wall pinning alter wave speed/amplitude, therefore in general interface constraints modify wave dynamics and can shift dispersion."];
  "N24" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given constraints modify wave dynamics and plastron interfaces are supported/pinned by micropillars, therefore capillary waves traveling on a plastron should depend on micropillar/contact-line characteristics."];
  "N25" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given immersion traps air between micropillars supporting water and this configuration is called a plastron/Cassie state, therefore the studied microstructured interface is a plastron (Cassie) gas–water interface."];
  "N26" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given the plastron depletes/changes over time and wave features can be used to infer interface state, therefore monitoring capillary-wave response could explain/track depletion and wetting evolution remotely."];
  "N27" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given mechanical waves carry medium properties and that measuring wave features can be used to infer interface state, therefore commonly measuring wave propagation enables inference of medium/interface properties (remote sensing principle)."];
  "N28" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given plastrons are mainly assessed optically and existing acoustics alternatives are limited, therefore a different remote, dynamics-accessible approach is needed."];
  "N29" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given a plastron is a gas–water interface and capillary waves are perturbations of a liquid–gas interface, therefore capillary waves can propagate on the plastron interface."];
  "N3" [label="(14,0,0) Currently, in shallow water (h << λ), a no-slip bottom boundary slows waves and increases their amplitude (shoaling-related effects)."];
  "N35" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given intermediate and Wenzel wetting states exist and monitoring should detect/distinguish these states, therefore a wave-based monitoring approach should target discriminating plastron, intermediate, and Wenzel configurations."];
  "N35r" [label="(0,0,0) If the plastron can be lost (Wenzel) and intermediate wetting states exist, then monitoring methods should detect and distinguish these wetting states over time."];
  "N4" [label="(19,0,0) Currently, most existing water-wave literature assumes the gas phase above the interface is boundary-free and inviscid."];
  "N5" [label="(20,0,0) Currently, systematic studies of capillary waves on interfaces pinned to an array of microstructures are lacking."];
  "N6" [label="(21,0,0) Currently, superhydrophobicity can facilitate an interface pinned to microstructures (superior water repellency supports such conditions)."];
  "N7" [label="(24,0,0) Currently, when immersed, microstructures (micropillars) can trap a thin air layer and support the water interface on pillar tops."];
  "N8" [label="(25,0,0) Currently, the trapped air layer supported by micropillars is called a plastron (Cassie state)."];
  "N9" [label="(27,0,0) Currently, the plastron can deplete via gas dissolution or pressure-induced impalement."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Develop and evaluate an ultrasound-induced capillary-wave approach on superhydrophobic micropillar plastrons to investigate how contact-line density and pillar height affect wave dispersion and to test whether wave behavior can remotely monitor plastron integrity and intermediate wetting states.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A research proposal is supported when a motivated physical platform, controllable experimental variables, a plausible measurement principle, and a relevant sensing objective jointly define what should be developed and evaluated."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) The need for a remote, dynamics-accessible plastron-assessment approach, together with the possibility that capillary-wave response can track plastron depletion and wetting evolution, motivates investigating the sensing potential of these waves."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Varying three-phase contact-line density and pillar height is a coherent way to test how controllable microstructure/pinning parameters affect capillary-wave behavior on a plastron."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) An ultrasound-driven capillary-wave configuration on a superhydrophobic plastron is a plausible experimental platform for investigating a remote, dynamics-accessible plastron-assessment approach."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) Because most existing water-wave literature assumes a boundary-free inviscid gas phase, while immersed micropillars can trap a thin air layer supporting the water interface, studying capillary waves on a plastron is motivated as a configuration outside that common assumption."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) Since wave-based monitoring should distinguish plastron, intermediate, and Wenzel configurations, and capillary-wave response could track plastron depletion and wetting evolution remotely, the sensing objective is well motivated."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) A well-motivated wave-based sensing objective aligns with the need for a remote, dynamics-accessible plastron-assessment approach, supporting its pursuit as part of the research proposal."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Because interface constraints can alter wave dynamics and measurable dispersion, microstructure/contact-line constraints on a plastron motivate testing whether capillary-wave dispersion changes with pinning conditions."];
  "N5" -> "N18" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
